  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe main weather story this week will be the late season heat\nwave with strong 594-596 decameter 500 mb high over the TN \nriver valley and into central/southern IL during mid week. Will \nlikely see afternoon heat indices peak from 100-105F through \nmuch of the week with 20-40% chance of seeing heat indices peak \nabove 105F today through Friday. Highs in the mid to upper 90s \ntoday through Fri with areas from Springfield to Robinson sw \nnear 100F. Springfield already reached 97F yesterday and only 2 \ndegrees from the record high of 99F. We posted record highs in \nthe climate section below for today through Thu. We continued \nthe heat advisory across CWA today through Thu afternoon, but \nmay need to be extended it into Friday or even Saturday if \nconvection holds off and have less cloud cover. \n\nA frontal boundary meandering over parts of Iowa and WI into \nThu to keep IL in the warm sector with hot humid tropical air \nmass staying in place. Convection chances appear to stay north \nof I-80 through Wed night, then have 20-40% pops over northern \nIL just north of CWA on Thu as a short wave trof passes by to our \nnorth. SPC Day3 Outlook has marginal risk of severe storms for wind \nand hail for late Thu/Thu night ne of a Canton to Lincoln to Terre \nHaute line. We have good instability Thu afternoon/evening, though \nbest forcing and bulk shear will be ne of CWA. We have slight chance \nof convection Friday into Saturday, mainly over ne CWA and eastern \nIL. A shortwave trough move eastward across the upper MS river \nvalley and into the Great Lakes region Wednesday night and Thursday, \nthough the brunt of its convection appears to pass north of central \nIL. A stronger short wave moves se across the Midwest and IL during \nthis holiday weekend as strong mid/upper level ridge retrogrades \nback into the Great Plains, putting IL in a NW upper level flow \nregime. A backdoor cold front could slip through central IL by \nSunday and may bring 20-30% chance of convection from Friday through \nSunday morning (highest pops Sat night). Hot temperatures in the 90s \nexpected through Saturday, to cool a bit into the upper 80s/lower \n90s early next week though still a good 10 degrees warmer than \nnormal. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Sep\n8-14th 50-55% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral/southeast IL with precipitation trending near normal.",
